WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:03.250
Say Achilles wants to run a race.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:08.309
First he’d have to run 1/2 way, then half
the rest of the way then, then half of that

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:12.420
and so on... but no, this video isn’t about
zeno’s paradox.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.560
I just wanted to point out, to get to the
end, Achilles had to run through every fraction

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.269
of the length at some point.

00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:22.109
So here’s the question for this video.

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.130
Does Achilles just run from fraction to fraction?

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.819
If your first instinct is like mine, you might
say, of course not.

00:00:29.819 --> 00:00:34.840
He doesn’t jump from one spot to another;
he smoothly goes along all the points.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:40.450
But see, if he goes along all the fractions,
he doesn’t have to jump.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.340
He starts at 0, but what’s the closest fraction
to 0?

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.580
Well, pick any really small fraction, there’s
always another that’s closer, just pick

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.890
the one that’s half of it.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.190
Then pick one closer than that by looking
at the half way point again, and again and

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.620
again, to infinity.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.060
This means, that there is never any distance
between the fraction Achilles is on, and the

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.750
next one, so there’s never any jumps.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.530
Ok, so it’s looks like we’re done, Achilles
can move smoothly along the fractions.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.890
But see how we’ve introduced infinity here?

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:20.930
The total distance is just a finite number,
but for Achilles to move smoothly along it,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.030
he has to along every fraction, and there
are a lot of fractions.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:29.830
It’s not just that there’s an infinite
number of fractions along this line, but that,

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:34.960
even between any two fractions, no matter
how tiny the distance is between them, there

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.880
is an infinite number of more fractions.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.720
That’s an insane infinity.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.670
But don’t worry, there is way way more.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.130
When we think of writing a fraction out in
decimal, it’s either some finite string

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.340
of digits, or sometimes and infinite one.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.450
But not all infinite strings of numbers are
fractions.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.020
Of course there’s all the classic examples.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.950
We’ll call these numbers irrational, the
ones that can’t be written as fractions

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.950
but can be written as a huge string of numbers.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.060
We said the fractions had no gaps right?

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.700
But consider this.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.920
Pick some irrational number, any.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.890
Let’s say mine starts off like this.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:22.370
Then say that Achilles runs with a constant
speed, but at first he runs .7 of the way,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.600
which is a fraction, so he can do that.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.790
Then he runs 0.09 more, again, a fraction,
so it’s fine.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:34.510
Then 0.003, etc etc.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:38.239
Each bit of the race is a fraction, so he’s
allowed to run that much.

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:43.400
He will stop when he reaches the end of this
process- you might be saying but he never

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.370
will, there’s an infinite number of steps.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.860
But don’t forget, he’s running at a constant
speed.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.590
That means the amount of time he takes to
run these smaller amounts is tiny.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:59.990
So he’ll get there in a finite amount of
time, in fact in slightly less time than it

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.980
would take him to run .8 of the way.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.480
So gets there alright.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.650
But the question is, where is there?

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.319
It’s this irrational number.

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:15.140
If he could only land on fractions, we would
be fine through this process since he’s

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:20.540
always landing on a fraction, but gets to
the end and is on a point we didn’t think

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.770
existed- so does just dissapear?

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:29.010
Since that doesn’t seem reasonable, we’re
forced to accept that all these irrational

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.890
numbers are there too, between the fractions
numbers, even though we showed that there

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.879
are no gaps between the fractions.

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:42.500
The fractions and the irrationals are collectively
called the real numbers, because they’re

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.330
the one’s we need for real world.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:51.000
The fact that we need real numbers might seem
obvious now, but it was a long long process

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.390
in maths to invent the right numbers to support
reality.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:04:00.550
This problem really puzzled mathematicians:
Suppose that we want to know how fast achilles

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.260
is running.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.180
If we just want his average, we’d just get
the total distance, and divide it by his time.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.190
But say Achilles tends to change his speed
quite a lot during the race.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.959
You want to know exactly what his speed is
at a particular point.

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:17.329
Then what do you do?

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:23.100
A better estimate is start timing for a short
amount of time after he passed that point,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.370
and see how far he’s gone.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.970
Then find the average speed from that.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:32.770
But of course that’s not completely accurate
either, his speed may have changed a bit even

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.090
in that smaller time.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:40.790
You’d want to make it more accurate by picking
a smaller time still, and doing the same thing.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:46.761
But of course, no matter how small you pick
the time, this is never 100% accurate... but

00:04:46.761 --> 00:04:51.130
we kind of need to know he’s speed at that
point in order to do physics.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.570
Well, then Libniz and Newton came along with
a great idea.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:05:00.150
They said, why don’t you look at what happens
when the time gap is really small.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.830
Like basically zero.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.070
But not zero.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:04.630
But smaller than any number.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:07.790
Let’s call it an infinitesimal.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:12.110
Then do the same calculation with your infinitesimal
this time.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.110
Done.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.400
Problem solved.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.509
If that sounds like magic to you, then don’t
worry, it is, but they cheerfully accepted

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:21.730
dividing by 0 because, well it worked.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.250
No one knew why it worked though.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.720
But it eventually started to really bother
mathematicians.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.721
They didn’t like these weird infinitesimals,
and how they weren’t zero sometimes and

00:05:30.721 --> 00:05:35.740
they were later- I mean, that’s not very
rigourous and mathematical sounding.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:41.680
So they went and started upheaving big chunks
of maths to make it more rigourous, in the

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:47.240
hope they would eventually be able to explain
calculus without the magic.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.320
Their hunt lead them all the way back to the
numbers themselves.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.960
They realised that what they needed was the
real numbers.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.120
See this property that the real numbers had,
that if a sequence is getting closer and closer

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.610
together, that it is going to converge to
something, that’s exactly what you need.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.680
Let’s see why.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.680
Back to our speed calculation.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.250
If you do that process of averaging, each
time halfing the time that you use, and repeat

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:19.639
to infinity, you get a long sequence of average
times.

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:22.000
Let’s say something like this.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.509
You can see that the numbers get closer.

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:31.150
But since we’re dealing with real numbers,
we know that this is all going to some number.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:37.520
Whatever that number is, define that to be
the speed of Achilles at the point.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.800
So real numbers saved the day.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.410
But not without a price.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:46.610
We saw that, even in a finite amount of space,
there is an infinite number of fractions,

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:51.510
and then an infinite number of irrationals
slotted between them.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:55.610
That is much more infinity then you might
have imagined fitting in there.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:56.890
But the plot thickens...

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:02.300
See, mathematicians where happy to discard
all those nasty infinitesimals from maths.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:09.380
But in the 60s, a mathematician discovered
that you can reformulate all of calculus completely

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:10.669
rigourously...

00:07:10.669 --> 00:07:13.320
using infinitesimals.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.900
Using that method, that dividing by zero but
not really dividing by zero thing is completely

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.199
valid.

00:07:20.199 --> 00:07:26.210
But instead of getting rid of real numbers
to do this, you actually add in another infinite

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:31.940
set of numbers, called the hyperreals, that
slot into the gaps between the reals.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.669
But that’s a story for another time.

00:07:34.669 --> 00:07:40.410
The conclusion of this video is that we have
an infinite number of numbers but

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.560
But, just because your universe is finite
in size, doesn’t mean it’s totally finite.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:44.990
Physics still needs a lot of infinity to support
it.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.740
I’ll explain why getting Achilles from A
to B requires you to assume there are way

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.419
more numbers in this distance than you might
